# The loop a boss fight puts the reader in. The point of the picture is the arrow that goes back,
# because every other part of the pipeline runs once and this is the part that repeats.
#
# The three files across the top are written before the reader arrives. The three boxes below are
# what happens once they do.

title How a boss fight is graded

size 980 660

box json  40  70 260 100 "boss.json" "the title, the brief and" "the answers, named"
box sol  360  70 260 100 "solution.cs" "the worked answer, run on" "every build"
box ans  680  70 260 100 "answers.txt" "one hash per answer," "generated, never typed"

box stub   40 240 260 100 "boss.cs" "yours. print one line" "per answer and run it"
box grade 400 240 260 100 "xray boss" "runs your file, hashes" "what it printed, compares"
box says  400 400 260 100 "what you get back" "which answers are wrong" "and what you printed"

arrow json sol
arrow sol ans
arrow ans grade "compared against"
arrow stub grade
arrow grade says
arrow says stub "go again"

note guard 40 530 900 70 "The build runs boss.cs too and requires it to fail. A file that already passes is a fight with nothing in it," "and that happens when a later edit to the solution quietly makes the stub right. A green build looks the same."
